  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13939873/assets/67dc6176a774b0dcb803a8e0935e2cc7c4241f635b18b7466fc530da8320cfe0.png)\n\nThe figure shows the graph of $y=f(x).$ Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{(xf(x))}^{3}.$",
  "worked_solution": "Applying the power rule and the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{(xf(x))}^{3} & =({lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{3})\\cdot ({lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)^{3}) \\\\  & =({lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{3})\\cdot {({lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x))}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we get that ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=3.$ Therefore, the above becomes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{(xf(x))}^{3} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{3}\\cdot {(3)}^{3} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{3}\\cdot 27 \\\\  & =27\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{3}$ is power function with an odd positive power and therefore has the property that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{3}=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the positive constant $27$ does not affect the limit at infinity. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}27\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{3} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{3} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{(xf(x))}^{3}=-\\infty .\n$$",
